        "\n",
        "**What would be the challenges to design and layout a logic gate as a standard cell?**\n",
        "\n",
        "From the perspective of the library / foundry designer we have learnt, the below challenges have been highlighted:\n",
        "* Yield: to maximise the yield, thereby optimising the unit economics each process will have to be extensively tested and parameterised (for example: temperature, chemical concentration, time). However optimised the process, random defects are inevitable, for example dust can cause failure via a short circuit. Fine tuning for a new technology can ramp up yields from 50 to 90% in the first years.\n",
        "* Complex interconnects and stacking: interconnects are separated by dielectric material which contribute to parasitic capacitance (proportional to the area that is parallel to each other). Given the large number of interconnects designers need to keep track of the effect of a metal's resistance and capacitance in relation to the performance of the device (i.e. switching speed of the circuit)\n",
        "* Geometric constraints: fitting logic gates within a fixed cell height with standardized $V_{DD}/V_{SS}$ rail heights and pin positions accessible to automated routers.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What are the changes for the cell under evolutions of the fabrication technologies?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Driven by Moore's law the planar transistor architecture has evolved wiht almost all chip manufacturers building integrated circuits with **FinFET** transistors. However, beyond the 3nm technology node, FinFETs suffer from quantum tunelling effects (current leakage) which has led to chip manufacturers like Samsung, Intel and TSMC announcing the **Gate-All-Around** (GAA or nanosheet) transistors built from a stack of nanosheets having lower voltage thresholds (energy efficiency) and leakages (reliability). Further scaling gains could be achieved by the GAA forksheet architecture and the **Complementary FET** (CFET) transistor. The CFET eliminates the lateral gaps between n-to-p reducing cell layout areas signficantly for logic gates. The trade-off of enhanced density includes the expense of process complexity, in particular; to contact the source and drains of transistors. Sub-2nm nodes, we have learnt about backside power delivery networks (BSPDN) which moves $V_{DD}$ and $V_{SS}$ freeing the frontside for signal routing and I/O pin access from inside the cell.  \n",
